window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-06-3", "source": "2017年06月真题·第3套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, Suppose you are asked to give advice on whether to attend college at home or abroad, write an essay to state your opinion. You are required to write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "When it comes to choosing a college, I firmly believe that studying abroad is the wiser choice for ambitious\nyoung people today. While a domestic university offers comfort and lower costs, the growth that comes from\nstepping into an unfamiliar world outweighs these conveniences.\nTo begin with, an overseas campus exposes students to genuine cultural diversity. Sitting in seminars with\nclassmates from a dozen countries, one learns to defend ideas and respect differences, skills that no textbook at\nhome can fully teach. What matters more, living far from family compels students to cook, budget and solve\nproblems alone, forging an independence that pampered domestic students often lack. Admittedly, tuition\nabroad is expensive; yet the language fluency and global vision gained are assets that repay the cost for\na lifetime.\nIn my view, therefore, the discomfort of studying abroad is exactly what makes it valuable. For those willing to\nleave their comfort zone, an overseas degree is an investment well worth making."}, "translation": {"prompt": "唐朝始于618年，终于907年，是中国历史上最灿烂的时期。经过近三百年的发展，唐代中国成为世界上最繁荣的强国，其首都长安是当时世界上最大的都市。这一时期，经济发达、商业繁荣、社会秩序稳定，甚至边境也对外开放。随着城市化和财富的增加，艺术和文学也繁荣起来。李白和杜甫是以作品简洁自然而著称的诗人。他们的诗歌打动了学者和普通人的心。即使在今天，他们的许多诗歌仍广为儿童及成人阅读背诵。", "sample": "The Tang Dynasty, which began in 618 and ended in 907, was the most splendid period in Chinese history. After nearly three centuries of development, Tang China became the most prosperous power in the world, and its capital Chang'an was the largest city in the world at that time. During this period, the economy was developed, commerce flourished, social order was stable, and even the borders were open to the outside world. With the growth of urbanization and wealth, art and literature flourished as well. Li Bai and Du Fu were poets known for the simplicity and naturalness of their works. Their poems touched the hearts of both scholars and ordinary people. Even today, many of their poems are still widely read and recited by both children and adults."}, "listeningUrl": null, "clozePassage": "Let's all stop judging people who talk to themselves. New research says that those who can't seem to keep their inner monologues(独白) in are actually more likely to stay on task, remain  __26__  better and show improved perception capabilities. Not bad, really, for some extra muttering.\nAccording to a series of experiments published in the Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology by professors Gary Lupyan and Daniel Swignley, the act of using verbal clues to  __27__  mental pictures helps people function quicker.\nIn one experiment, they showed pictures of various objects to twenty  __28__  and asked them to find just one of those, a banana. Half were  __29__  to repeat out loud what they were looking for and the other half kept their lips  __30__  . Those who talked to themselves found the banana slightly faster than those who didn't, the researchers say. In other experiments, Lupyan and Swignley found that  __31__  the name of a common product when on the hunt for it helped quicken someone's pace, but talking about uncommon items showed no advantage and slowed you down.\nCommon research has long held that talking themselves through a task helps children learn, although doing so when you've  __32__  matured is not a great sign of  __33__  . The two professors hope to refute that idea,  __34__  that just as when kids walk themselves through a process, adults can benefit from using language not just to communicate, but also to help \"augment thinking\".\nOf course, you are still encouraged to keep the talking at library tones and, whatever you do, keep the information you share simple, like a grocery list. At any  __35__  , there's still such a thing as too much information.", "matchIntro": "Rich Children and Poor Ones Are Raised Very Differently", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "The lives of children from rich and poor American families look more different than ever before."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Well-off families are ruled by calendars, with children enrolled in ballet, soccer and after-school programs, according to a new Pew Research Center survey. There are usually two parents, who spend a lot of time reading to children and worrying about their anxiety levels and hectic schedules."}, {"label": "C", "text": "In poor families, meanwhile, children tend to spend their time at home or with extended family. They are more likely to grow up in neighborhoods that their parents say aren't great for raising children, and their parents worry about them getting shot, beaten up or in trouble with the law."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The class differences in child rearing are growing—a symptom of widening inequality with far-reaching consequences. Different upbringings set children on different paths and can deepen socioeconomic divisions, especially because education is strongly linked to earnings. Children grow up learning the skills to succeed in their socioeconomic stratum(阶层), but not necessarily others."}, {"label": "E", "text": "\"Early childhood experiences can be very consequential for children's long-term social, emotional and cognitive development,\" said Sean Reardon, professor of poverty and inequality in education at Stanford University. \"And because those influence educational success and later earnings, early childhood experiences cast a lifelong shadow.\" The cycle continues: Poorer parents have less time and fewer resources to invest in their children, which can leave children less prepared for school and work, which leads to lower earnings."}, {"label": "F", "text": "American parents want similar things for their children, the Pew report and past research have found: for them to be healthy and happy, honest and ethical, caring and compassionate. There is no best parenting style or philosophy, researchers say, and across income groups, 92% of parents say they are doing a good job at raising their children. Yet they are doing it quite differently. Middle-class and higher-income parents see their children as projects in need of careful cultivation, says Annette Lareau, whose groundbreaking research on the topic was published in her book Unequal Childhoods: Class, Race and Family Life. They try to develop their skills through close supervision and organized activities, and teach children to question authority figures and navigate elite institutions."}, {"label": "G", "text": "Working-class parents, meanwhile, believe their children will naturally thrive, and give them far greater independence and time for free play. They are taught to be compliant and respectful to adults. There are benefits to both approaches. Working-class children are happier, more independent, complain less and are closer with family members, Ms. Lareau found. Higher-income children are more likely to declare boredom and expect their parents to solve their problems. Yet later on, the more affluent children end up in college and on the way to the middle class, while working-class children tend to struggle. Children from higher-income families are likely to have the skills to navigate bureaucracies and succeed in schools and workplaces, Ms. Lareau said."}, {"label": "H", "text": "\"Do all parents want the most success for their children? Absolutely,\" she said. \"Do some strategies give children more advantages than others in institutions? Probably they do. Will parents be damaging children if they have one fewer organized activity? No, I really doubt it.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Social scientists say the differences arise in part because low-income parents have less money to spend on music class or preschool, and less flexible schedules to take children to museums or attend school events. Extracurricular activities reflect the differences in child rearing in the Pew survey, which was of a nationally representative sample of 1,807 parents. Of families earning more than $75,000 a year, 84% say their children have participated in organized sports over the past year, 64% have done volunteer work and 62% have taken lessons in music, dance or art. Of families earning less than $30,000, 59% of children have done sports, 37% have volunteered and 41% have taken arts classes."}, {"label": "J", "text": "Especially in affluent families, children start young. Nearly half of high-earning, college-graduate parents enrolled their children in arts classes before they were 5, compared with one-fifth of low-income, less-educated parents. Nonetheless, 20% of well-off parents say their children's schedules are too hectic, compared with 8% of poorer parents."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Another example is reading aloud, which studies have shown gives children bigger vocabularies and better reading comprehension in school. 71% of parents with a college degree say they do it every day, compared with 33% of those with a high school diploma or less. White parents are more likely than others to read to their children daily, as are married parents. Most affluent parents enroll their children in preschool or day care, while low-income parents are more likely to depend on family members. Discipline techniques vary by education level: 8% of those with a postgraduate degree say they often beat their children, compared with 22% of those with a high school degree or less."}, {"label": "L", "text": "The survey also probed attitudes and anxieties. Interestingly, parents' attitudes toward education do not seem to reflect their own educational background as much as a belief in the importance of education for upward mobility. Most American parents say they are not concerned about their children's grades as long as they work hard. But 50% of poor parents say it is extremely important to them that their children earn a college degree, compared with 39% of wealthier parents."}, {"label": "M", "text": "Less-educated parents, and poorer and black and Latino parents are more likely to believe that there is no such thing as too much involvement in a child's education. Parents who are white, wealthy or college-educated say too much involvement can be bad. Parental anxieties reflect their circumstances. High-earning parents are much more likely to say they live in a good neighborhood for raising children. While bullying is parents' greatest concern overall, nearly half of low-income parents worry their child will get shot, compared with one-fifth of high-income parents. They are more worried about their children being depressed or anxious."}, {"label": "N", "text": "In the Pew survey, middle-class families earning between $30,000 and $75,000 a year fell right between working-class and high-earning parents on issues like the quality of their neighborhood for raising children, participation in extracurricular activities and involvement in their children's education."}, {"label": "O", "text": "Children were not always raised so differently. The achievement gap between children from high- and low-income families is 30–40% larger among children born in 2001 than those born 25 years earlier, according to Mr. Reardon's research. People used to live near people of different income levels; neighborhoods are now more segregated by income. More than a quarter of children live in single-parent households—a historic high, according to Pew—and these children are three times as likely to live in poverty as those who live with married parents. Meanwhile, growing income inequality has coincided with the increasing importance of a college degree for earning a middle-class wage."}, {"label": "P", "text": "Yet there are recent signs that the gap could be starting to shrink. In the past decade, even as income inequality has grown, some of the socioeconomic differences in parenting, like reading to children and going to libraries, have narrowed."}, {"label": "Q", "text": "Public policies aimed at young children have helped, including public preschool programs and reading initiatives. Addressing differences in the earliest years, it seems, could reduce inequality in the next generation."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their facilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus.\nIn an email sent Monday to college presidents in the Tennessee Board of Regents system, outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by the state. Morgan said those findings—which included data from the system's 13 community colleges, 27 technical colleges and six universities—were part of the decision not to move forward with Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings in an effort to save money.\n\"While these numbers are still being validated by the state, we feel any adjustments they might suggest will be immaterial,\" Morgan wrote to the presidents. \"System institutions are operating very efficiently based on this analysis, raising the question of the value of pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative.\"\nWorkers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits. Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not been finalized.\nMorgan notified the Haslam administration of his decision to opt out in a letter sent last week. That letter, which includes several concerns Morgan has with the plan, was originally obtained by The Commercial Appeal in Memphis.\nIn an email statement from the state's Office of Customer Focused Government, which is examining the possibility of outsourcing, spokeswoman Michelle R. Martin said officials were still working to analyze the data from the Board of Regents. Data on management expenses at the college system and in other state departments will be part of a \"business justification\" the state will use as officials deliberate the specifics of an outsourcing plan.\n\"The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the end of February,\" Martin said. \"At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to be completed.\"\nMorgan's comments on outsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one of Haslam's plans for higher education in Tennessee. Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate governing boards for each of them. In his resignation letter, Morgan called the reorganization \"unworkable\".", "47": "Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their facilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus.\nIn an email sent Monday to college presidents in the Tennessee Board of Regents system, outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by the state. Morgan said those findings—which included data from the system's 13 community colleges, 27 technical colleges and six universities—were part of the decision not to move forward with Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings in an effort to save money.\n\"While these numbers are still being validated by the state, we feel any adjustments they might suggest will be immaterial,\" Morgan wrote to the presidents. \"System institutions are operating very efficiently based on this analysis, raising the question of the value of pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative.\"\nWorkers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits. Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not been finalized.\nMorgan notified the Haslam administration of his decision to opt out in a letter sent last week. That letter, which includes several concerns Morgan has with the plan, was originally obtained by The Commercial Appeal in Memphis.\nIn an email statement from the state's Office of Customer Focused Government, which is examining the possibility of outsourcing, spokeswoman Michelle R. Martin said officials were still working to analyze the data from the Board of Regents. Data on management expenses at the college system and in other state departments will be part of a \"business justification\" the state will use as officials deliberate the specifics of an outsourcing plan.\n\"The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the end of February,\" Martin said. \"At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to be completed.\"\nMorgan's comments on outsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one of Haslam's plans for higher education in Tennessee. Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate governing boards for each of them. In his resignation letter, Morgan called the reorganization \"unworkable\".", "48": "Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their facilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus.\nIn an email sent Monday to college presidents in the Tennessee Board of Regents system, outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by the state. Morgan said those findings—which included data from the system's 13 community colleges, 27 technical colleges and six universities—were part of the decision not to move forward with Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings in an effort to save money.\n\"While these numbers are still being validated by the state, we feel any adjustments they might suggest will be immaterial,\" Morgan wrote to the presidents. \"System institutions are operating very efficiently based on this analysis, raising the question of the value of pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative.\"\nWorkers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits. Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not been finalized.\nMorgan notified the Haslam administration of his decision to opt out in a letter sent last week. That letter, which includes several concerns Morgan has with the plan, was originally obtained by The Commercial Appeal in Memphis.\nIn an email statement from the state's Office of Customer Focused Government, which is examining the possibility of outsourcing, spokeswoman Michelle R. Martin said officials were still working to analyze the data from the Board of Regents. Data on management expenses at the college system and in other state departments will be part of a \"business justification\" the state will use as officials deliberate the specifics of an outsourcing plan.\n\"The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the end of February,\" Martin said. \"At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to be completed.\"\nMorgan's comments on outsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one of Haslam's plans for higher education in Tennessee. Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate governing boards for each of them. In his resignation letter, Morgan called the reorganization \"unworkable\".", "49": "Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their facilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus.\nIn an email sent Monday to college presidents in the Tennessee Board of Regents system, outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by the state. Morgan said those findings—which included data from the system's 13 community colleges, 27 technical colleges and six universities—were part of the decision not to move forward with Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings in an effort to save money.\n\"While these numbers are still being validated by the state, we feel any adjustments they might suggest will be immaterial,\" Morgan wrote to the presidents. \"System institutions are operating very efficiently based on this analysis, raising the question of the value of pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative.\"\nWorkers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits. Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not been finalized.\nMorgan notified the Haslam administration of his decision to opt out in a letter sent last week. That letter, which includes several concerns Morgan has with the plan, was originally obtained by The Commercial Appeal in Memphis.\nIn an email statement from the state's Office of Customer Focused Government, which is examining the possibility of outsourcing, spokeswoman Michelle R. Martin said officials were still working to analyze the data from the Board of Regents. Data on management expenses at the college system and in other state departments will be part of a \"business justification\" the state will use as officials deliberate the specifics of an outsourcing plan.\n\"The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the end of February,\" Martin said. \"At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to be completed.\"\nMorgan's comments on outsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one of Haslam's plans for higher education in Tennessee. Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate governing boards for each of them. In his resignation letter, Morgan called the reorganization \"unworkable\".", "50": "Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their facilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus.\nIn an email sent Monday to college presidents in the Tennessee Board of Regents system, outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by the state. Morgan said those findings—which included data from the system's 13 community colleges, 27 technical colleges and six universities—were part of the decision not to move forward with Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings in an effort to save money.\n\"While these numbers are still being validated by the state, we feel any adjustments they might suggest will be immaterial,\" Morgan wrote to the presidents. \"System institutions are operating very efficiently based on this analysis, raising the question of the value of pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative.\"\nWorkers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits. Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not been finalized.\nMorgan notified the Haslam administration of his decision to opt out in a letter sent last week. That letter, which includes several concerns Morgan has with the plan, was originally obtained by The Commercial Appeal in Memphis.\nIn an email statement from the state's Office of Customer Focused Government, which is examining the possibility of outsourcing, spokeswoman Michelle R. Martin said officials were still working to analyze the data from the Board of Regents. Data on management expenses at the college system and in other state departments will be part of a \"business justification\" the state will use as officials deliberate the specifics of an outsourcing plan.\n\"The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the end of February,\" Martin said. \"At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to be completed.\"\nMorgan's comments on outsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one of Haslam's plans for higher education in Tennessee. Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate governing boards for each of them. In his resignation letter, Morgan called the reorganization \"unworkable\".", "51": "Beginning in the late sixteenth century, it became fashionable for young aristocrats to visit Paris, Venice, Florence, and above all, Rome, as the culmination(终极) of their classical education. Thus was born the idea of the Grand Tour, a practice which introduced Englishmen, Germans, Scandinavians, and also Americans to the art and culture of France and Italy for the next 300 years. Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\nThe Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art. The German traveler Johann Winckelmann pioneered the field of art history with his comprehensive study of Greek and Roman sculpture; he was portrayed by his friend Anton Raphael Mengs at the beginning of his long residence in Rome. Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly intentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs of their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces.\nLondon was a frequent starting point for Grand Tourists, and Paris a compulsory destination; many traveled to the Netherlands, some to Switzerland and Germany, and a very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey. The essential place to visit, however, was Italy. The British traveler Charles Thompson spoke for many Grand Tourists when in 1744 he described himself as \"being impatiently desirous of viewing a country so famous in history, a country which once gave laws to the world, and which is at present the greatest school of music and painting, contains the noblest productions of sculpture and architecture, and is filled with cabinets of rarities, and collections of all kinds of historical relics\". Within Italy, the great focus was Rome, whose ancient ruins and more recent achievements were shown to every Grand Tourist. Panini's Ancient Rome and Modern Rome represent the sights most prized, including celebrated Greco-Roman statues and views of famous ruins, fountains, and churches. Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private collections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for their own collections. In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative(唤起回忆的) ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens.", "52": "Beginning in the late sixteenth century, it became fashionable for young aristocrats to visit Paris, Venice, Florence, and above all, Rome, as the culmination(终极) of their classical education. Thus was born the idea of the Grand Tour, a practice which introduced Englishmen, Germans, Scandinavians, and also Americans to the art and culture of France and Italy for the next 300 years. Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\nThe Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art. The German traveler Johann Winckelmann pioneered the field of art history with his comprehensive study of Greek and Roman sculpture; he was portrayed by his friend Anton Raphael Mengs at the beginning of his long residence in Rome. Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly intentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs of their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces.\nLondon was a frequent starting point for Grand Tourists, and Paris a compulsory destination; many traveled to the Netherlands, some to Switzerland and Germany, and a very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey. The essential place to visit, however, was Italy. The British traveler Charles Thompson spoke for many Grand Tourists when in 1744 he described himself as \"being impatiently desirous of viewing a country so famous in history, a country which once gave laws to the world, and which is at present the greatest school of music and painting, contains the noblest productions of sculpture and architecture, and is filled with cabinets of rarities, and collections of all kinds of historical relics\". Within Italy, the great focus was Rome, whose ancient ruins and more recent achievements were shown to every Grand Tourist. Panini's Ancient Rome and Modern Rome represent the sights most prized, including celebrated Greco-Roman statues and views of famous ruins, fountains, and churches. Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private collections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for their own collections. In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative(唤起回忆的) ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens.", "53": "Beginning in the late sixteenth century, it became fashionable for young aristocrats to visit Paris, Venice, Florence, and above all, Rome, as the culmination(终极) of their classical education. Thus was born the idea of the Grand Tour, a practice which introduced Englishmen, Germans, Scandinavians, and also Americans to the art and culture of France and Italy for the next 300 years. Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\nThe Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art. The German traveler Johann Winckelmann pioneered the field of art history with his comprehensive study of Greek and Roman sculpture; he was portrayed by his friend Anton Raphael Mengs at the beginning of his long residence in Rome. Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly intentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs of their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces.\nLondon was a frequent starting point for Grand Tourists, and Paris a compulsory destination; many traveled to the Netherlands, some to Switzerland and Germany, and a very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey. The essential place to visit, however, was Italy. The British traveler Charles Thompson spoke for many Grand Tourists when in 1744 he described himself as \"being impatiently desirous of viewing a country so famous in history, a country which once gave laws to the world, and which is at present the greatest school of music and painting, contains the noblest productions of sculpture and architecture, and is filled with cabinets of rarities, and collections of all kinds of historical relics\". Within Italy, the great focus was Rome, whose ancient ruins and more recent achievements were shown to every Grand Tourist. Panini's Ancient Rome and Modern Rome represent the sights most prized, including celebrated Greco-Roman statues and views of famous ruins, fountains, and churches. Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private collections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for their own collections. In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative(唤起回忆的) ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens.", "54": "Beginning in the late sixteenth century, it became fashionable for young aristocrats to visit Paris, Venice, Florence, and above all, Rome, as the culmination(终极) of their classical education. Thus was born the idea of the Grand Tour, a practice which introduced Englishmen, Germans, Scandinavians, and also Americans to the art and culture of France and Italy for the next 300 years. Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\nThe Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art. The German traveler Johann Winckelmann pioneered the field of art history with his comprehensive study of Greek and Roman sculpture; he was portrayed by his friend Anton Raphael Mengs at the beginning of his long residence in Rome. Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly intentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs of their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces.\nLondon was a frequent starting point for Grand Tourists, and Paris a compulsory destination; many traveled to the Netherlands, some to Switzerland and Germany, and a very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey. The essential place to visit, however, was Italy. The British traveler Charles Thompson spoke for many Grand Tourists when in 1744 he described himself as \"being impatiently desirous of viewing a country so famous in history, a country which once gave laws to the world, and which is at present the greatest school of music and painting, contains the noblest productions of sculpture and architecture, and is filled with cabinets of rarities, and collections of all kinds of historical relics\". Within Italy, the great focus was Rome, whose ancient ruins and more recent achievements were shown to every Grand Tourist. Panini's Ancient Rome and Modern Rome represent the sights most prized, including celebrated Greco-Roman statues and views of famous ruins, fountains, and churches. Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private collections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for their own collections. In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative(唤起回忆的) ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens.", "55": "Beginning in the late sixteenth century, it became fashionable for young aristocrats to visit Paris, Venice, Florence, and above all, Rome, as the culmination(终极) of their classical education. Thus was born the idea of the Grand Tour, a practice which introduced Englishmen, Germans, Scandinavians, and also Americans to the art and culture of France and Italy for the next 300 years. Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\nThe Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art. The German traveler Johann Winckelmann pioneered the field of art history with his comprehensive study of Greek and Roman sculpture; he was portrayed by his friend Anton Raphael Mengs at the beginning of his long residence in Rome. Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly intentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs of their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces.\nLondon was a frequent starting point for Grand Tourists, and Paris a compulsory destination; many traveled to the Netherlands, some to Switzerland and Germany, and a very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey. The essential place to visit, however, was Italy. The British traveler Charles Thompson spoke for many Grand Tourists when in 1744 he described himself as \"being impatiently desirous of viewing a country so famous in history, a country which once gave laws to the world, and which is at present the greatest school of music and painting, contains the noblest productions of sculpture and architecture, and is filled with cabinets of rarities, and collections of all kinds of historical relics\". Within Italy, the great focus was Rome, whose ancient ruins and more recent achievements were shown to every Grand Tourist. Panini's Ancient Rome and Modern Rome represent the sights most prized, including celebrated Greco-Roman statues and views of famous ruins, fountains, and churches. Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private collections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for their own collections. In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative(唤起回忆的) ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-06-3-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) focused\n形容词\n【词性槽】remain + 空，空作表语\n【依据】stay focused 是固定表达\nfocused 与前文 stay on task（保持专心于任务）并列，stay on task / remain focused\n是英语里描述“专注”的常见连用；且 focused 强调“注意力集中”，与后文 show improved\nperception capabilities（感知能力提升）的逻辑链一致。\n【竞争词】E) dedicated dedicated 偏“长期投入/奉献”，不指即时注意力\ndedicated 多指“对事业/工作长期投入、奉献”，常用 dedicated to sth.；而本句讨论的是自言\n自语者“当下任务中”的专注状态，与“奉献”含义不匹配；且 remain dedicated 通常需要后跟 to\n+ 对象，本句没有。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·remain + 空，空作表语"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) trigger\n动词\n【词性槽】to 后接动词原形\n【依据】trigger mental pictures = 触发心理图像\ntrigger 是“激发/触发”，正面或中性色彩，可与 mental pictures 搭配表“调出脑海中的图\n像”；与全文“说出关键词加速大脑反应”的主旨一致：用语言线索去“触发”大脑里的图片，加\n快搜索。\n【竞争词】G) incur incur 多接负面后果，感情色彩与文意冲突\nincur 常接 loss、debt、anger、cost 等负面结果，意为“招致（不好的事）”；而 mental\npictures 是中性/积极的认知产物，且本句在说自言自语的**正面**效果，感情色彩与 incur\n反向。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— 感情色彩反向最易误选。incur 词性、句法位都对得上 to\n__(27)__ mental pictures；考生若不熟悉“incur 多接负面后果”的搭配色彩，极易当作“引\n起”就填进去。防坑一句话：incur 接坏事，trigger 接反应。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·to 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) volunteers\n名词\n【词性槽】数词 twenty 后必跟可数名词复数\n【依据】心理学实验里被试称作 volunteers\n原文 In one experiment, they showed pictures of various objects to twenty __(28)__\nand asked them to find just one of those, a banana 描述的是实验场景，被试是**自愿\n参加实验的人**，对应 volunteers；而且第二段已铺垫 a series of experiments，符合“招\n募志愿者做实验”的常见表达。\n【竞争词】K) spectators 观众用于赛事/演出，不用于实验被试\nspectators 指体育比赛、表演的“观众”，是被动观看，不参与任务；而本句要求 asked them to\nfind just one of those, a banana，被试要主动搜索香蕉，是实验参与者而非旁观者，词义指向\n不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·数词 twenty 后必跟可数名词复数"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) instructed\n动词\n【词性槽】be 动词后 + to 不定式 → 过去分词作被动\n【依据】instruct sb. to do 是固定搭配\ninstructed 表“被指示/被要求做某事”，搭配“be instructed to do”是最常用结构。原文 Half\nwere instructed to repeat out loud what they were looking for and the other half\nkept their lips __(30)__ 表明实验员让一半志愿者出声重复，正是被指示的动作。\n【竞争词】E) dedicated 搭配是 dedicated to 而非 dedicated to do\ndedicated 表“致力于”，固定搭配是 be dedicated to + 名词/动名词（to 是介词），而本句 to\nrepeat 是不定式；且“被致力于重复”逻辑不通，不符合实验员“下指令”的语境。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词后 + to 不定式 → 过去"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) sealed\n形容词\n【词性槽】keep + 宾语 + 空，空作宾补\n【依据】sealed lips = 紧闭双唇\n另一半被试不出声，即“双唇紧闭”，sealed 与上一句 Half were instructed to repeat out\nloud 形成“出声 vs 不出声”的鲜明对照，逻辑链清晰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·keep + 宾语 + 空，空作宾补"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) uttering\n动词\n【词性槽】found that 引导的主语缺空，need 动名词\n【依据】uttering the name = 说出名字\nuttering 意为“说出（一个声音/词）”，与上下文“talked to themselves”“repeat out\nloud”一脉相承——本段全在讲“出声说”能加速任务；utter the name of a product 即“说\n出某商品的名字”，与“when on the hunt for it 找东西时”逻辑顺畅。\n【竞争词】D) claiming claim 偏“主张/索取”，不指简单说出名字\nclaiming 意为“宣称/主张/索取”，常接观点或权利（claim that …/claim a prize），用 claim\nthe name of a product 不符合英语习惯；且全段讨论的是“出声重复名字”的发音动作，不是“主\n张商品名”的言论行为。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·found that 引导的主语缺空，n"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) apparently\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词 've 与过去分词 matured 之间 → 副词\n【依据】apparently 修饰整句表“显然”\napparently “显然地/看上去”，修饰 you've matured，表“你显然已经成熟了”——作者用这\n一带轻微反讽语气的副词，引出下句“成年人再自言自语就被认为不聪明”的世俗偏见，与第\n四段后半文要 refute that idea 形成铺垫。\n【竞争词】I) obscurely obscurely 偏“晦涩地”，与 matured 搭配语义违和\nobscurely 意为“含糊地/不为人知地”，常修饰表达或处境（如 obscurely worded），不修饰\nmature 这种描述“个人成长状态”的动词；“晦涩地成熟”逻辑不通。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·助动词 've 与过去分词 mature"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) brilliance\n名词\n【词性槽】of 后接名词作介词宾语\n【依据】not a great sign of brilliance = 不是聪明的标志\nbrilliance “聪颖”是世俗偏见里“成熟成年人应当展现”的特质；原句意在转述偏见——“成年\n人还自言自语，看起来不是聪明的表现”。与后文 The two professors hope to refute that\nidea 中两教授要反驳的“成年人自言自语 = 不聪明”的观念严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】B) arrogance arrogance 偏“傲慢”，与上下文“聪明/不聪明”话题反向\narrogance “傲慢”是负面性格特质；本句要表达的是“不是聪明（智力）的标志”而非“不是傲慢\n（性格）的标志”，且“自言自语 → 不傲慢”的逻辑与全文“是否聪明”的讨论完全错位。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·of 后接名词作介词宾语"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) claiming\n动词\n【词性槽】句末逗号后 + 空 + that 从句，分词作伴随状语\n【依据】claim that … = 主张某观点\nclaiming 表“主张/宣称”，能接 that 从句，恰好引出两位教授“想反驳的对立观点”的具体内\n容：adults can benefit from using language not just to communicate, but also to\nhelp \"augment thinking\"。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·句末逗号后 + 空 + that 从句，"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) apparently", "B) arrogance", "C) brilliance", "D) claiming", "E) dedicated", "F) focused", "G) incur", "H) instructed", "I) obscurely", "J) sealed", "K) spectators", "L) trigger", "M) uttering", "N) volume", "O) volunteers"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) volume\n名词\n【词性槽】At any + 空 → 固定搭配里的名词\n【依据】at any volume 承接上文 library tones\nvolume “音量”与本段开篇 keep the talking at library tones（用图书馆里的低音量）紧密\n呼应——作者从“说话音量”过渡到“无论以何种音量”都要注意信息别太多。注：虽然惯用语\n是 at any rate，但本段以 volume 收束，正是利用“音量”这一关键词把段落语境串起来的写\n作选择，rate 也不在词库中。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 B) —— 孤立看习语 at any rate 易走偏。考生若死记 at any rate\n是“无论如何”却发现 rate 不在词库，可能慌乱去试 arrogance 等抽象名词；防坑一句话：\n词库里不放 rate，就回段首找关键词——本段 library tones 直接锁 volume。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·At any + 空 → 固定搭配里的名"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Working-class parents teach their children to be obedient and show respect to adults.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】工人阶级 + 顺从尊重成人\n【定位】They are taught to be compliant and respectful to adults.\n他们被教育要顺从、要尊重成人。\n【改写】Working-class parents teach their children ↔\nWorking-class parents, meanwhile, believe their children will naturally thrive 与 They are\ntaught to be compliant and respectful to adults\n代词 They 回指 Working-class parents 的孩子\nobedient and show respect to adults ↔ compliant and respectful to adults\n同义词替换", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "American parents, whether rich or poor, have similar expectations of their children despite different ways of parenting.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】贫富父母期望相似\n【定位】American parents want similar things for their children, the Pew report and past research have\nfound: for them to be healthy and happy, honest and ethical, caring and compassionate.\nPew 报告与既往研究发现：美国父母对孩子的期望相似，都希望孩子健康快乐、诚实有德、\n有爱心、有同情心。\n【改写】American parents, whether rich or poor, have similar expectations of their children ↔\nAmerican parents want similar things for their children\nwant similar things = have similar expectations\nwhether rich or poor ↔ across income groups, 92% of parents say they are doing a good job\nacross income groups = 不论贫富\ndespite different ways of parenting ↔ Yet they are doing it quite differently\nYet 让步 = despite 让步\n【辨邻】 B B 仅描述富裕家庭日程满满、担心 anxiety levels and hectic schedules，根本没有把\n贫富父母的期望放在一起比较。\nWell-off families are ruled by calendars, with children enrolled in ballet, soccer and after-\nschool programs", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "While rich parents are more concerned with their children's psychological well-being, poor parents are more worried about their children's safety.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】心理健康 vs 安全\n【定位】While bullying is parents' greatest concern overall, nearly half of low-income parents worry\ntheir child will get shot, compared with one-fifth of high-income parents. They are more\nworried about their children being depressed or anxious.\n尽管欺凌是父母总体上最大的担忧，但近一半低收入父母担心孩子被枪击，高收入父母只有\n五分之一。高收入父母更担心孩子抑郁或焦虑。\n【改写】rich parents are more concerned with their children's psychological well-being ↔\nThey are more worried about their children being depressed or anxious\ndepressed or anxious = psychological well-being\npoor parents are more worried about their children's safety ↔\nnearly half of low-income parents worry their child will get shot\nget shot 是 safety 担忧的具体表现\n【辨邻】 C C 只描写贫困家庭父母担心孩子 getting shot, beaten up or in trouble with the\nlaw，根本没写富裕父母担心孩子心理健康，单边无法承担陈述句的并列对比。\ntheir parents worry about them getting shot, beaten up or in trouble with the law\nB B 写富裕父母关注 anxiety levels and hectic schedules，是单边描述，没有把贫富两\n类焦虑并列。\nworrying about their anxiety levels and hectic schedules", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "The increasing differences in child rearing between rich and poor families reflect growing social inequality.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】育儿差异反映社会不平等\n【定位】The class differences in child rearing are growing—a symptom of widening inequality with\nfar-reaching consequences.\n育儿中的阶级差异正在扩大——这是不平等扩大、影响深远的一个征兆。\n【改写】The increasing differences in child rearing between rich and poor families ↔\nThe class differences in child rearing are growing\nclass differences = differences between rich and poor\nreflect growing social inequality ↔ a symptom of widening inequality\nsymptom of = reflect\n【辨邻】 O O 讲 Children were not always raised so differently 与 30–40% larger 的成绩差距\n对比，主线是今昔变化与按收入分居，没有'育儿差异 = 不平等征兆'这层反映关系的\n断言。\nThe achievement gap between children from high- and low-income families is 30–40% larger\namong children born in 2001 than those born 25 years earlier\nE E 强调 Early childhood experiences can be very consequential 与 cast a lifelong\nshadow，落点是早期经历影响长远，不是把育儿差异作为社会不平等的征兆。\nPoorer parents have less time and fewer resources to invest in their children, which can leave\nchildren less prepared for school and work, which leads to lower earnings", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Parenting approaches of working-class and affluent families both have advantages.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】两种育儿方式都有优势\n【定位】There are benefits to both approaches.\n两种方式都有好处。\n【改写】Parenting approaches of working-class and affluent families ↔\nWorking-class parents, meanwhile, believe their children will naturally thrive 与 Higher-\nincome children are more likely to declare boredom\nboth approaches 指上下文两种育儿方式\nboth have advantages ↔ There are benefits to both approaches\nbenefits 同义于 advantages\n【辨邻】 H H 表态 Do some strategies give children more advantages than others in\ninstitutions? Probably they do，是承认某些策略更占优，并非'两种方式都有好处'的\n对称评价。\nDo some strategies give children more advantages than others in institutions? Probably they do.\nF F 强调 There is no best parenting style or philosophy 与跨阶层 92% 自评良好，并\n未论证两种方式各自的优点。\nThere is no best parenting style or philosophy, researchers say, and across income groups, 92% of\nparents say they are doing a good job at raising their children.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "Higher-income families and working-class families now tend to live in different neighborhoods.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】高低收入家庭住不同邻里\n【定位】People used to live near people of different income levels; neighborhoods are now more\nsegregated by income.\n过去人们与不同收入的人住在一起；如今邻里更多按收入隔离。\n【改写】Higher-income families and working-class families ↔\nPeople used to live near people of different income levels\ndifferent income levels = 高低收入两类家庭\nnow tend to live in different neighborhoods ↔\nneighborhoods are now more segregated by income\nneighborhoods 按收入隔离 = 住到不同邻里\n【辨邻】 C C 写贫困家庭孩子 grow up in neighborhoods that their parents say aren't great\nfor raising children，重点是治安差，不是高低收入分别住在不同社区。\nThey are more likely to grow up in neighborhoods that their parents say aren't great for\nraising children\nM M 写 High-earning parents are much more likely to say they live in a good\nneighborhood for raising children，只是评价，不是按收入分居的事实陈述。\nHigh-earning parents are much more likely to say they live in a good neighborhood for\nraising children", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "Physical punishment is used much less by well-educated parents.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】体罚 + 高学历父母\n【定位】Discipline techniques vary by education level: 8% of those with a postgraduate degree say\nthey often beat their children, compared with 22% of those with a high school degree or less.\n管教方式随父母学历不同而不同：研究生学历父母有 8% 表示经常打孩子，而高中及以下学\n历的父母这一比例是 22%。\n【改写】Physical punishment ↔ beat their children\nbeat 是 physical punishment 的具体形式\nused much less by well-educated parents ↔\n8% of those with a postgraduate degree 与 22% of those with a high school degree or less\n8% < 22% 直接支撑 much less", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Ms. Lareau doesn't believe participating in fewer after-class activities will negatively affect children's development.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】专名 Lareau + 减少课外活动\n【定位】Will parents be damaging children if they have one fewer organized activity? No, I really\ndoubt it.\n如果孩子少参加一项有组织的活动，父母会因此伤害孩子吗？不会，我真的不这么认为。\n【改写】Ms. Lareau doesn't believe ↔ I really doubt it\nI doubt = doesn't believe 含蓄否定\nparticipating in fewer after-class activities ↔ they have one fewer organized activity\norganized activity = after-class activity\nwill negatively affect children's development ↔ parents be damaging children\ndamaging children = 负面影响发展\n【辨邻】 G G 引述 Ms. Lareau said 时讲 There are benefits to both approaches 与 Higher-\nincome children are more likely to declare boredom，没有针对'减少课外活动'的\n判断。\nHigher-income children are more likely to declare boredom and expect their parents to solve\ntheir problems.\nF F 写 Middle-class and higher-income parents see their children as projects in\nneed of careful cultivation，落点在'精心栽培'，没有否定减少活动的伤害。\nMiddle-class and higher-income parents see their children as projects in need of careful cultivation", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "Wealthy parents are concerned about their children's mental health and busy schedules.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】富裕父母 + 心理健康 + 忙碌日程\n【定位】There are usually two parents, who spend a lot of time reading to children and worrying about\ntheir anxiety levels and hectic schedules.\n通常有两位父母，他们花大量时间给孩子读书，并担心孩子的焦虑水平和忙碌日程。\n【改写】Wealthy parents ↔ Well-off families are ruled by calendars\nWell-off = Wealthy\ntheir children's mental health and busy schedules ↔ their anxiety levels and hectic schedules\nanxiety = mental health, hectic = busy\n【辨邻】 M M 提到 depressed or anxious，但没有把忙碌日程作为富裕父母的担忧并列写出，无\n法独立支撑陈述句的两条并列担忧。\nThey are more worried about their children being depressed or anxious\nJ J 写 20% of well-off parents say their children's schedules are too hectic，是统计\n自评，不是描述富裕父母同时担心心理与日程。\n20% of well-off parents say their children's schedules are too hectic, compared with 8% of\npoorer parents.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Some socioeconomic differences in child rearing have shrunk in the past ten years.", "options": null, "answer": "P", "analysis": "【答案】P 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】近十年 + 差异缩小\n【定位】In the past decade, even as income inequality has grown, some of the socioeconomic\ndifferences in parenting, like reading to children and going to libraries, have narrowed.\n在过去十年里，即使收入不平等扩大了，育儿中一些社会经济差异（例如给孩子读书、去图\n书馆）反而收窄了。\n【改写】Some socioeconomic differences in child rearing ↔\nsome of the socioeconomic differences in parenting\nchild rearing = parenting\nhave shrunk in the past ten years ↔ In the past decade ... have narrowed\ndecade = ten years, narrow = shrink\n【辨邻】 O O 写 30–40% larger，是差距扩大；与 shrunk 方向完全相反，是典型的'正话反\n说'干扰。\nThe achievement gap between children from high- and low-income families is 30–40% larger\namong children born in 2001 than those born 25 years earlier\nQ Q 写 could reduce inequality in the next generation，时态指向未来或假设，不是\nP 段的 'have narrowed' 现在完成时事实陈述。\nAddressing differences in the earliest years, it seems, could reduce inequality in the next generation.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about the decision of technical and community colleges in Tennessee?", "options": ["A) It is backed by a campus spending analysis.", "B) It has been flatly rejected by the governor.", "C) It has neglected their faculty's demands.", "D) It will improve their financial situation."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about the decision of technical and community colleges in\nTennessee? 问的是关于这个决定我们从文中得知什么，是对原文事实的客观转述，不带\ninfer / imply。\n【拆句】What do we learn about the decision of technical and community colleges in\nTennessee? 是一个直接特殊疑问句，about 后面带一个长名词短语，里面 of technical\nand community colleges in Tennessee 是 the decision 的两层后置修饰（of 短语 + in\n短语）；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Tennessee's technical and community colleges will not outsource(外包) management of their\nfacilities to a private company, a decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of\nspending at each campus.\n田纳西州的技术学院与社区学院不会把它们的设施管理外包给私营公司，一位领导者说这一\n决定有 \"对各校园开支的一份分析\" 作支撑。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\na decision one leader said was bolstered by an analysis of spending at each campus ↔\nIt is backed by a campus spending analysis.\n选项 It is backed by a campus spending analysis. 把 bolster 换成 back，把 of 短语\n压成复合名词，语义和预判 \"决定有开支分析作支撑\" 一一对应。\nB) 看清谁拒绝了谁\nIt has been flatly rejected by the governor. 的 rejected by the governor 与\nGovernor Bill Haslam's proposal to privatize management of state buildings 中\n州长 \"提出外包\" 的事实相反——是 Morgan 不接受州长的方案，不是州长否决学院的\n决定。\nC) 无中生有\nIt has neglected their faculty's demands. 中 faculty's demands（教师诉求）在原文\n没有任何对应：文中提到的是 Workers' advocates（工人权益倡导者），并非 faculty\n（教师），也未出现 demands 这一指控。\nD) 过度推断\nIt will improve their financial situation. 把改善财务状况说成学院决定的结果；原文里\nin an effort to save money 是州长想 \"为州里省钱\" 才提出外包的动机，并未说不外包\n能改善学院财务。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the campus spending analysis reveal?", "options": ["A) Private companies play a big role in campus management.", "B) Facilities management by colleges is more cost-effective.", "C) Facilities management has greatly improved in recent years.", "D) Colleges exercise full control over their own financial affairs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What does the campus spending analysis reveal? 问的是分析揭示了什么客观事实，仍\n属对原文结论句的转述。\n【拆句】What does the campus spending analysis reveal? 是 What 作宾语前置的特殊疑问句，\n主语是 the campus spending analysis，谓语 reveal；结构清晰，无陷阱。\n【定位】outgoing Chancellor John Morgan said an internal analysis showed that each campus'\nspending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards identified by\nthe state.\n即将离任的校长 John Morgan 说，一份内部分析显示，每一所校园在设施管理上的开支都\n远低于该州认定的行业标准。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nPrivate companies play a big role in campus management. 与原文方向直接相反\n——分析得出的结论恰恰是 \"不需要外包给私企\"，raising the question of the value\nof pursuing a broad scale outsourcing initiative 是在反问外包的必要性。\nB) ✓ 正确\neach campus' spending on facilities management fell well below the industry standards ↔\nFacilities management by colleges is more cost-effective.\n\"开支低于业内标准 + 学院自己在管\" 两段信息合起来就是 \"由学院来管反而更省钱\"，与\n选项 Facilities management by colleges is more cost-effective. 完全等值。\nC) 无中生有\nFacilities management has greatly improved in recent years. 中 in recent years 的\n时间纵向比较，全文没有任何依据；原文只做 \"当前校园开支 vs 当前业内标准\" 的横向\n比较，未涉及历史改进。\nD) 以偏概全\nColleges exercise full control over their own financial affairs. 中 full control over\nfinancial affairs 的全权说法在原文没依据；原文只谈一笔具体开支（设施管理）低于业\n内标准，并未谈学院整体财权的归属。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Workers' supporters argue that Bill Haslam's proposal would _________.", "options": ["A) Deprive colleges of the right to manage their facilities", "B) Make workers less motivated in performing duties", "C) Render a number of campus workers jobless", "D) Lead to the privatization of campus facilities"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】Workers' supporters argue that Bill Haslam's proposal would _________. 留空让你补出\nWorkers' supporters 的论点内容，即原文对应论点的同义转述。\n【拆句】Workers' supporters argue that Bill Haslam's proposal would _________. 是一个简单复\n合句：主句主语 Workers' supporters，谓语 argue；that 引导宾语从句，从句里 Bill\nHaslam's proposal 作主语、would 后接选项补出的动词原形；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Workers' advocates have criticized Haslam's plan, saying it would mean some campus\nworkers would lose their jobs or benefits.\n工人权益倡导者批评 Haslam 的方案，称这会意味着一些校园工人失去工作或福利。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\ndeprive colleges of the right to manage their facilities 把后果说成 \"剥夺学院自管\n权\"，原文 Haslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the\noutsourcing plan 反而强调学校可自由进出。\nB) 偷换概念\nmake workers less motivated in performing duties 提到 \"工作积极性下降\"，原文从\n未谈及 motivation 维度；倡导者担心的是 \"失业 / 失福利\" 这种实体损失，不是士气。\nC) ✓ 正确\nsome campus workers would lose their jobs or benefits ↔\nrender a number of campus workers jobless\n原文给的是 \"丢工作 OR 丢福利\" 两个并列后果，选项 render a number of campus\nworkers jobless 命中其中 \"丢工作\" 这一支；六级选 \"原文也直说过\" 的那一项，没\n毛病。\nD) 答非所问\nlead to the privatization of campus facilities 把 \"私有化校园设施\" 说成倡导者批评的\n后果；但 privatize 本来就是 Haslam 计划本身（Governor Bill Haslam's proposal to\nprivatize management of state buildings），不是倡导者另外担心的后果。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What do we learn from the state spokeswoman's response to John Morgan's decision?", "options": ["A) The outsourcing plan is not yet finalized.", "B) The outsourcing plan will be implemented.", "C) The state officials are confident about the outsourcing plan.", "D) The college spending analysis justifies the outsourcing plan."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the state spokeswoman's response to John Morgan's\ndecision? 问的是发言人回应中我们读到了什么客观事实，仍是原文转述。\n【拆句】What do we learn from the state spokeswoman's response to John Morgan's\ndecision? 是特殊疑问句，from 引出短语作状语，里面 the state spokeswoman's\nresponse 是核心名词，后跟 to John Morgan's decision 的介词短语作后置修饰，限定是\n\"对谁的决定的回应\"；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its\nbusiness justification and expects to have that completed and available to the public at the\nend of February. At this time there is nothing to take action on since the analysis has yet to\nbe completed.\n州里负责设施管理项目的团队仍在制定其业务可行性报告，预计 2 月底完成并对外公开。眼\n下没有可执行的事项，因为分析还没完成。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nThe state's facilities management project team is still in the process of developing its business\njustification\n↔ The outsourcing plan is not yet finalized.\nHaslam has said colleges would be free to opt in or out of the outsourcing plan, which has not\nbeen finalized.\n↔ The outsourcing plan is not yet finalized.\n发言人原话强调 \"仍在制定 / 没东西可执行\"，与第 4 段 which has not been finalized\n完全合拍，对应选项 The outsourcing plan is not yet finalized.。\nB) 过度推断\nThe outsourcing plan will be implemented. 把 \"将会实施\" 当作发言人态度，原文反\n而是 At this time there is nothing to take action on——现在无可执行，并未承诺一\n定实施。\nC) 无中生有\nThe state officials are confident about the outsourcing plan. 用 confident 强加情\n绪色彩，原文发言人通篇是 \"还在做、还没定\" 的中性陈述，未现任何信心表态。\nD) 与原文相反\nThe college spending analysis justifies the outsourcing plan. 让学院开支分析去 \"证\n成外包\"，与第 2 段 fell well below the industry standards 给出的结论方向相反——\n分析得出的是 \"无需外包\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why did John Morgan decide to resign?", "options": ["A) He had lost confidence in the Tennessee state government.", "B) He disagreed with the governor on higher education policies.", "C) He thought the state's outsourcing proposal was simply unworkable.", "D) He opposed the governor's plan to reconstruct the college board system."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why did John Morgan decide to resign? 问的是原因，原文有 because of 显式给出，属\n对原因句的转述。\n【拆句】Why did John Morgan decide to resign? 是 Why 起手的特殊疑问句，结构平铺直叙，\ndecide to resign 是 \"动词 + 不定式\" 的常规搭配；无陷阱。\n【定位】Morgan said last week that he would retire at the end of January because of the governor's\nproposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system and create separate\ngoverning boards for each of them.\nMorgan 上周说他将在 1 月底退休，原因是州长提议把董事会系统下的 6 所大学拆出来，\n各自成立独立的治理委员会。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\nHe had lost confidence in the Tennessee state government. 把原因从 \"反对州长\n某具体方案\" 扩大成 \"对州政府整体失去信心\"，原文从未谈及他对州政府整体的信任\n状态。\nB) 以偏概全\nHe disagreed with the governor on higher education policies. 中 higher\neducation policies 是复数泛指 \"高等教育政策\" 整体；原文 Morgan's comments on\noutsourcing mark the second time this month that he has come out against one\nof Haslam's plans 只表明 \"第二次反对州长其中一项方案\"，仍是单一政策维度。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe thought the state's outsourcing proposal was simply unworkable. 把辞职原因\n说成 \"觉得外包方案不可行\"，但原文 In his resignation letter, Morgan called the\nreorganization \"unworkable\". 中 unworkable 修饰的是 the reorganization（重组）\n而不是 outsourcing；辞职原因明确是 because of the governor's proposal to split\noff six universities，即重组，不是外包。\nD) ✓ 正确\nbecause of the governor's proposal to split off six universities of the Board of Regents system\nand create separate governing boards for each of them\n↔ He opposed the governor's plan to reconstruct the college board system.\n原文交代他辞职的直接原因就是州长这一拆分提案，辞职这个行为本身即 oppose 的最\n强表达，与选项 He opposed the governor's plan to reconstruct the college board\nsystem. 等值。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What is said about the Grand Tour?", "options": ["A) It was fashionable among young people of the time.", "B) It was unaffordable for ordinary people.", "C) It produced some famous European artists.", "D) It made a compulsory part of college education."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What is said about the Grand Tour 直接问关于这件事的某个陈述，是对原文细节的事实\n性核对，不要求归纳整篇也不要求推断。\n【拆句】What is said about the Grand Tour? 是 What 引导的一般疑问句，谓语用被动 is said，\n主语是疑问词 What。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Travel was arduous and costly throughout the period, possible only for a privileged class—\nthe same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and patrons of the arts.\n整个时期，旅行都既艰辛又昂贵，只有特权阶层才负担得起——也正是这一阶层产生了绅士\n科学家、作家、古董鉴赏家和艺术赞助人。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nIt was fashionable among young people of the time 把原文的 young aristocrats\n（年轻贵族）偷偷换成 young people（年轻人）。原文限定是年轻贵族这个特权小群\n体，并未说在所有年轻人中流行。\nB) ✓ 正确\npossible only for a privileged class ↔ unaffordable for ordinary people\n预判是「只有少数人去得起」，B 选项 unaffordable for ordinary people 正是这句话\n从「少数人可以」翻成「多数人不可以」的对称否定，与定位句严丝合缝。\nC) 无中生有\nIt produced some famous European artists 说 Grand Tour 培养出了著名艺术家。原\n文 the same that produced gentlemen scientists, authors, antique experts, and\npatrons of the arts 说的是「特权阶层」产生了这些人，且这些人是科学家、作家、\n古董专家、艺术赞助人，不是 artists；而且产生主语是「阶层」，不是 Grand Tour\n本身。\nD) 偷换概念\nIt made a compulsory part of college education 用 college education 替换原文的\nclassical education。前者指大学教育，后者指对古希腊罗马经典的学习；而且原文用\n的是 fashionable（流行的），不是 compulsory（必修的）。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What did Grand Tourists have in common?", "options": ["A) They had much geographic knowledge.", "B) They were courageous and venturesome.", "C) They were versed in literature and interested in art.", "D) They had enough travel and outdoor-life experience."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What did Grand Tourists have in common 问的是一个客观共性事实，仍属于「原文写了\n就有、没写就没」的细节核对。\n【拆句】What did Grand Tourists have in common? 一般疑问句，have in common 是固定搭配\nhave something in common（有共同点）的中间挖空形式。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】The Grand Tourist was typically a young man with a thorough grounding in Greek and Latin\nliterature as well as some leisure time, some means, and some interest in art.\nGrand Tourist 通常是一位对希腊和拉丁文学有透彻基础、并有一定闲暇时间、一定经济能\n力以及对艺术抱有兴趣的年轻人。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nThey had much geographic knowledge（具备丰富的地理知识）在原文中找不到对应\n——原文给的是古典文学、闲、钱、艺术兴趣，未提地理知识。\nB) 以偏概全\nThey were courageous and venturesome（勇敢而富冒险精神）只对应第 3 段提到的\na very few adventurers to Spain, Greece, or Turkey，即极少数冒险家，而不是\nGrand Tourist 群体的共性。\nC) ✓ 正确\na thorough grounding in Greek and Latin literature ↔ versed in literature\nsome interest in art ↔ interested in art\n预判要求同时覆盖「古典文学功底 + 艺术兴趣」，C 项 versed in literature and\ninterested in art 两段并列恰好对应两点，无遗漏。\nD) 无中生有\nThey had enough travel and outdoor-life experience（有充分的旅行和户外经验）\n原文没说。原文只在第 1 段说 Travel was arduous and costly——旅行艰难，反而暗\n示他们从未做过这类艰苦旅行。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "How did Grand Tourists benefit from their travel?", "options": ["A) They found inspiration in the world's greatest masterpieces.", "B) They got a better understanding of early human civilization.", "C) They developed an interest in the origin of Modern art forms.", "D) They gained some knowledge of classical art and architecture."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】How did Grand Tourists benefit from their travel 问的是一个客观事实——旅行带给他\n们什么收获，是细节核对题。\n【拆句】How did Grand Tourists benefit from their travel? How 引导的一般疑问句，benefit\nfrom 是固定搭配（从…中获益）。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Most Grand Tourists, however, stayed for briefer periods and set out with less scholarly\nintentions, accompanied by a teacher or guardian, and expected to return home with souvenirs\nof their travels as well as an understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to\ngreat masterpieces.\n然而大多数 Grand Tourists 停留时间较短、出发时学术目的也较弱，由一位教师或监护人\n陪同，并预期带着旅行纪念品以及通过接触杰作而形成的对艺术与建筑的理解回到家中。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nThey found inspiration in the world's greatest masterpieces（从最伟大的杰作中找\n到灵感）原文只说 exposure to great masterpieces（接触杰作），并未说找到了灵\n感；找到灵感是创作者的反应，原文是观众式的学习。\nB) 偷换概念\nThey got a better understanding of early human civilization（对早期人类文明有了\n更好理解）原文谈的是 art and architecture，特别是古希腊罗马的艺术与建筑，不是泛\n泛的「早期人类文明」。early human civilization 通常指史前或更广义的远古文明。\nC) 与原文相反\nThey developed an interest in the origin of Modern art forms（对现代艺术形式的\n起源产生兴趣）原文谈的全部是 classical/Greco-Roman 古典艺术，与「现代艺术」在\n时代上正好相反，且未提「起源研究」。\nD) ✓ 正确\nan understanding of art and architecture formed by exposure to great masterpieces ↔\nThey gained some knowledge of classical art and architecture\nas the culmination of their classical education ↔ classical art and architecture\n预判要求「获得艺术与建筑的认识」，D They gained some knowledge of classical\nart and architecture 三个语义点完整覆盖：gained = formed/return with、\nknowledge = understanding、art and architecture 原词，命中度最高。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why did many Grand Tourists visit the private collections?", "options": ["A) They could buy unique souvenirs there to take back home.", "B) Europe hardly had any museums before the 19th century.", "C) They found the antiques there more valuable.", "D) Private collections were of greater variety."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why did many Grand Tourists visit the private collections 问的是一个具体原因，原文\n应有 since/because 之类的因果句，是细节题而非推断题。\n【拆句】Why did many Grand Tourists visit the private collections? Why 引导的一般疑问句，\nvisit 是谓语动词。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Since there were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth\ncentury, Grand Tourists often saw paintings and sculptures by gaining admission to private\ncollections, and many were eager to acquire examples of Greco-Roman and Italian art for\ntheir own collections.\n由于 18 世纪末以前整个欧洲几乎没有什么博物馆，Grand Tourists 往往通过取得进入私人\n收藏的许可来看画作与雕塑，许多人还急切地希望为自己的收藏获取希腊罗马和意大利艺术\n的样本。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nThey could buy unique souvenirs there to take back home（他们可以在那里买独特\n的纪念品带回家）原文说的是 gaining admission to private collections（取得参观许\n可），并非购物；纪念品 souvenirs 是第 2 段的话题，与「为何参观私人收藏」无关。\nB) ✓ 正确\nthere were few museums anywhere in Europe before the close of the eighteenth century ↔\nEurope hardly had any museums before the 19th century\n预判「当时几乎没博物馆」与 B Europe hardly had any museums before the 19th\ncentury 一一对应，连时间表达都完成了 the close of the eighteenth century → the\n19th century 的对称转换。\nC) 无中生有\nThey found the antiques there more valuable（他们发现那里的古董更有价值）原文\n没有比较——既未说私人收藏比哪里的更有价值，也没有 valuable 这种判断。\nD) 无中生有\nPrivate collections were of greater variety（私人收藏种类更丰富）原文未提「种类\n多」或与其他来源做对比；原文只说很多人「急于为自己的收藏获取样本」，与「种类\n更多」无关。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-06-3-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "How did the Grand Tour influence the architecture in England?", "options": ["A) There appeared more and more Roman-style buildings.", "B) Many aristocrats began to move into Roman-style villas.", "C) Aristocrats' country houses all had Roman-style gardens.", "D) Italian architects were hired to design houses and gardens."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】How did the Grand Tour influence the architecture in England 问的是一个原文已写出\n的具体影响，仍走细节核对，不是要归纳全篇主旨。\n【拆句】How did the Grand Tour influence the architecture in England? How 引导的一般疑问\n句，influence 是谓语，the architecture in England 是宾语，in England 修饰\narchitecture。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】In England, where architecture was increasingly seen as an aristocratic pursuit, noblemen\noften applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative\nruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens.\n在英格兰，建筑越来越被视为贵族的爱好，贵族们常常把他们从威尼托地区帕拉第奥别墅以\n及罗马富于唤起力的废墟中学到的东西，运用到自己的乡间住宅和花园之中。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nnoblemen often applied what they learned from the villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the\nevocative ruins of Rome to their own country houses and gardens\n↔ There appeared more and more Roman-style buildings\nthe villas of Palladio in the Veneto and the evocative ruins of Rome ↔ Roman-style\n原文写「贵族把学到的罗马风格用到自家房子」，A There appeared more and more\nRoman-style buildings 是从结果角度对这件事的客观转述，对应得严丝合缝。\nB) 偷换概念\nMany aristocrats began to move into Roman-style villas（许多贵族开始搬进罗马风\n格的别墅）原文是把「从别墅中学到的」风格应用到自家英国住宅上，不是搬进意大利\n的别墅；move into 这一行为原文未提。\nC) 以偏概全\nAristocrats' country houses all had Roman-style gardens（贵族的乡间住宅都有罗\n马风格的花园）原文用 often applied（经常应用），是高频但非全部；选项 all 把高频\n升级成绝对全部，范围被偷偷放大。\nD) 无中生有\nItalian architects were hired to design houses and gardens（意大利建筑师被雇来\n设计房子和花园）原文说的是 noblemen 自己应用所学，未提雇佣意大利建筑师；hire\n这一动作整体属于杜撰。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}], "listeningSharedWith": "2017-06-1"});
